IV. READ-ONLY — EVIDENCE PRESERVATION AND REVIEW

Definition

Read-only access allows information to be examined without permitting the reviewer to modify the authoritative source record.

This is especially important when information may be used in research, regulatory review, institutional investigations, environmental monitoring, or public-interest reporting.

Read-only responsibilities

- View original records.
- Examine metadata.
- Compare source and derivative files.
- Inspect timestamps.
- Verify cryptographic hashes.
- Review analytical findings.
- Examine access history.
- Export authorized reports.

Read-only versus immutable

These terms are not interchangeable.

Term	Meaning

Read-only	A user or process cannot modify information through the granted interface
Immutable	A record cannot be changed under the defined storage and governance model
Append-only	New records may be added, but existing records are not overwritten
Version-controlled	Changes are represented through distinct versions and history
Cryptographically verifiable	A record can be compared against a recorded cryptographic integrity value


A read-only interface does not guarantee immutable storage.

A robust design should preserve the original source file and place every subsequent transformation into a separately identified derivative record.

For example:

SOURCE-001 → DERIVATIVE-001 → ANALYSIS-001 → REVIEW-001

Each record should reference its predecessor.


